        'slug' => 'mood-architecture-manic-hypomanic-depressive',
        'description' => 'Detailed educational reviewer covering Mood Architecture: Defining Manic, Hypomanic, and Depressive Episodes under DSM-5 criteria.',
        'overview' => "Mood Architecture: Defining Manic, Hypomanic, and Depressive Episodes represents the foundational building blocks of the Bipolar and Related Disorders diagnostic realm in DSM-5. In psychiatric classification, mood disorders are understood as complex neurobiological syndromes characterized by dysregulation in circadian rhythms, dopaminergic and glutamatergic signaling, and frontolimbic emotional processing circuits. Rather than reflecting transient emotional reactions to daily events, pathological mood episodes represent sustained, pervasive alterations in energy, activity, sleep architecture, and cognition.\n\nIn DSM-5, Bipolar and Related Disorders were separated from Depressive Disorders into their own dedicated diagnostic chapter. This pivotal reorganization recognizes that bipolar disorders occupy a distinct biological, genetic, and clinical bridge between schizophrenia spectrum disorders and unipolar depressive disorders. Family pedigree, genome-wide association, and neuroimaging studies consistently demonstrate that bipolar disorder shares substantial polygenic vulnerability and neurobiological architecture with schizophrenia, justifying its distinct taxonomic placement.\n\nA central structural requirement in DSM-5 is that mood elevation must be accompanied by persistent increases in activity or energy. While DSM-IV emphasized elevated, expansive, or irritable mood alone, DSM-5 elevated 'persistently increased activity or energy' to a mandatory core Criterion A requirement alongside mood disturbance. This vital refinement ensures that clinicians evaluate objective behavioral activation alongside subjective affective states.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "To construct an accurate diagnostic formulation, clinicians must distinguish between the three primary mood episode building blocks:\n\n1. Manic Episode: A distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood AND abnormally and persistently increased goal-directed activity or energy, lasting at least 1 week and present most of the day, nearly every day (or any duration if hospitalization is necessary). During this period, three (or more) of the following symptoms (four if the mood is only irritable) must be present to a significant degree and represent a noticeable change from usual behavior: (1) Inflated self-esteem or grandiosity; (2) Decreased need for sleep (e.g., feels rested after 3 hours); (3) More talkative than usual or pressure to keep talking; (4) Flight of ideas or subjective experience that thoughts are racing; (5) Distractibility; (6) Increase in goal-directed activity or psychomotor agitation; (7) Excessive involvement in activities that have a high potential for painful consequences (e.g., unrestrained buying sprees, sexual indiscretions, foolish business investments). Crucially, the mood disturbance must cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, or necessitate hospitalization to prevent harm, or involve psychotic features.\n\n2. Hypomanic Episode: Meets the identical symptom criteria as mania, but with two decisive operational differences: (1) Duration: The episode must last at least 4 consecutive days and be present most of the day, nearly every day; (2) Severity: The episode is associated with an unequivocal change in functioning that is uncharacteristic of the individual and observable by others, BUT is NOT severe enough to cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, does NOT necessitate hospitalization, and CANNOT include psychotic features. If hospitalization, marked impairment, or psychotic features occur, the episode is automatically classified as Manic, not Hypomanic.\n\n3. Major Depressive Episode: Requires five (or more) of nine symptoms present during the same 2-week period representing a change from previous functioning, with at least one symptom being either depressed mood or loss of interest/pleasure (anhedonia). Additional symptoms include significant weight/appetite change, insomnia/hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation/retardation, fatigue/loss of energy, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, diminished concentration/indecisiveness, and recurrent suicidal ideation or suicide attempts.",
        'specifiers' => "Mood episodes are characterized using standardized clinical specifiers: 'With anxious distress' (presence of feeling keyed up, tense, unusually restless, fear that something awful may happen), 'With mixed features' (presence of depressive symptoms during manic/hypomanic episodes, or manic symptoms during depressive episodes), 'With rapid cycling' (at least 4 mood episodes in the previous 12 months), 'With melancholic features', 'With atypical features', 'With psychotic features' (mood-congruent vs. mood-incongruent), 'With catatonia', and 'With peripartum onset'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Manic vs. Hypomanic Episode: Differentiated strictly by three criteria: (1) Duration (>= 1 week vs. >= 4 consecutive days); (2) Functional impact (marked impairment vs. no marked impairment); and (3) Hospitalization / Psychosis (if hospitalization or psychosis occurs, the episode is Manic by definition).\n\nBipolar Mood Elevation vs. Stimulant Intoxication: Cocaine, methamphetamine, or prescription stimulant misuse can produce euphoria, decreased sleep, and grandiosity; toxicology screening and detailed timeline analysis are required. Normal Productivity vs. Hypomania: Normal high productivity lacks the uncharacteristic, driven, and observable behavioral change that defines hypomanic episodes.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 29-year-old financial analyst who, over the past five days, has slept only two hours per night without feeling tired. Friends note that the analyst speaks at a rapid, unstoppable pace, jumping from business venture to venture (flight of ideas, pressured speech).\n\nThe analyst has committed $80,000 to unvetted overseas startups and is planning to purchase three luxury vehicles (excessive involvement in high-risk activities). The behavior causes severe workplace disruption and prompted emergency department evaluation due to marked impairment. Because the episode features severe functional impairment and has lasted five days with marked consequences, it satisfies criteria for a Manic Episode, confirming that any marked impairment elevates the episode above hypomania regardless of the 7-day timeline.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Bipolar I requires at least one lifetime Manic episode (>= 1 week or requiring hospitalization). Bipolar II requires at least one Hypomanic episode (>= 4 consecutive days) AND at least one Major Depressive episode, with NO history of full mania. If psychotic features, hospitalization, or marked functional impairment are present, the episode is MANIC, never hypomanic. Rapid cycling requires >= 4 mood episodes in a 12-month period. DSM-5 elevated 'persistently increased activity or energy' to a mandatory Criterion A requirement.",
        'key_concepts' => [
