        'description' => 'Diagnostic criteria, single manic episode sufficiency, severity grading, hospitalization rules, and psychotic features.',
        'overview' => "Bipolar I Disorder is the quintessential classical form of manic-depressive illness, defined fundamentally by the occurrence of at least one lifetime manic episode. A critical diagnostic principle in DSM-5 is that a single lifetime manic episode is both necessary and sufficient to establish a lifetime diagnosis of Bipolar I Disorder. While the vast majority of individuals with Bipolar I Disorder also experience major depressive episodes throughout their lives, a documented major depressive episode is not strictly required to assign the diagnosis.\n\nBipolar I Disorder carries profound morbidity, high rates of hospitalization, and significant suicide risk (estimated at 15 times that of the general population). The neurobiology involves profound disruption in frontostriatal and limbic networks, including hyperdopaminergic tone during manic phases, altered intracellular second-messenger signaling (such as protein kinase C and inositol cascades), and compromised circadian clock-gene expression.\n\nSeverity grading in Bipolar I Disorder reflects the degree of behavioral disorganization, occupational collapse, and potential for harm. Episodes with psychotic features represent medical emergencies that require acute pharmacological stabilization with mood stabilizers (e.g., lithium, valproate) and second-generation antipsychotics.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires that criteria for at least one manic episode have been met (>= 1 week duration of elevated/irritable mood and increased energy, with 3+ manic symptoms, or 4+ if irritable mood only, causing marked impairment or hospitalization).\n\nCriterion B mandates that the occurrence of the manic and major depressive episode(s) is not better explained by Schizoaffective Disorder, Schizophrenia, Schizophreniform Disorder, Delusional Disorder, or other specified psychotic spectrum disorders.\n\nCoding Rules: The diagnostic code for Bipolar I Disorder is determined by whether the current or most recent episode is Manic, Hypomanic, or Depressed, and whether it is Mild, Moderate, Severe, With psychotic features, In partial remission, or In full remission.",
        'specifiers' => "Severity grading for current manic episodes: (1) Mild (barely meets criteria, minimal functional disruption); (2) Moderate (significant increase in activity or impaired judgment); (3) Severe (requires continuous supervision to prevent physical harm to self or others).\n\nPsychotic specifiers: 'With psychotic features' (subdivided into 'With mood-congruent psychotic features' such as grandiose delusions of royal lineage or superpowers, versus 'With mood-incongruent psychotic features' such as persecutory delusions or voices unrelated to grandiose themes). Course specifiers include 'With rapid cycling' (>= 4 episodes in 12 months) and 'With anxious distress'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Bipolar I vs. Bipolar II Disorder: Bipolar I features full manic episodes (marked impairment, >= 1 week, or hospitalization); Bipolar II NEVER includes a manic episode (features hypomania and major depression only).\n\nBipolar I vs. Schizoaffective Disorder: In Schizoaffective Disorder, delusions or hallucinations must occur for >= 2 weeks in the absence of prominent mood episodes; in Bipolar I with psychotic features, psychosis occurs exclusively during active manic or depressive episodes.\n\nBipolar I vs. Major Depressive Disorder: If a patient with recurrent depression experiences even a single past manic episode, the diagnosis must be immediately reclassified from Major Depressive Disorder to Bipolar I Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves a 25-year-old individual who stopped sleeping four days ago, claiming to have received divine blueprints for a clean-energy engine. The individual has spent $45,000 on industrial components, is pacing relentlessly, speaking in pressured rhymes, and became combative when family members expressed concern.\n\nEmergency psychiatric hospitalization was initiated to ensure safety. Although symptoms have lasted only four days, the necessity of emergency hospitalization automatically fulfills the duration and severity criteria for a Manic Episode. Because criteria for mania are satisfied, the diagnosis is confirmed as Bipolar I Disorder, Current Episode Manic, Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: A single lifetime Manic episode establishes the diagnosis of Bipolar I Disorder; a major depressive episode is NOT required. Hospitalization satisfies the duration requirement regardless of whether 7 days have passed. If psychotic features are present, the episode is MANIC by definition. Suicide risk in Bipolar I is approximately 15 times higher than in the general population. Once a manic episode occurs, the diagnosis of unipolar Major Depressive Disorder can never be made again.",
