        'description' => 'Diagnostic dual requirement (hypomania + major depression), exclusion of mania, chronic depressive burden, and misdiagnosis risks.',
        'overview' => "Bipolar II Disorder is a distinct clinical entity characterized by the occurrence of at least one hypomanic episode AND at least one major depressive episode during the individual's lifetime, with the absolute absence of any lifetime manic episode. Historically and colloquially mischaracterized as a 'milder' form of bipolar disorder, empirical clinical data reveals that Bipolar II Disorder is associated with profound chronicity, severe depressive morbidity, high functional impairment, and substantial suicide completion rates.\n\nIn Bipolar II Disorder, patients spend the overwhelming majority of their ill time—often more than 75%—in debilitating, treatment-resistant depressive states rather than in hypomania. Hypomanic episodes are frequently experienced by the patient as periods of desirable high productivity, creativity, and energy, leading them to view hypomania as their 'normal' state. Consequently, patients almost exclusively present for clinical treatment during depressive episodes, rarely volunteering past hypomanic periods.\n\nThis clinical dynamic makes Bipolar II Disorder one of the most frequently misdiagnosed conditions in psychiatry. Patients are commonly misdiagnosed with unipolar Major Depressive Disorder, resulting in inappropriate antidepressant monotherapy that can trigger rapid cycling, mixed states, or treatment resistance.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires that criteria have been met for at least one hypomanic episode (>= 4 consecutive days of elevated/irritable mood and increased energy, with 3+ symptoms, observable change, NO marked impairment, NO hospitalization, NO psychosis) AND at least one major depressive episode (>= 2 weeks of 5+ depressive symptoms including depressed mood or anhedonia).\n\nCriterion B establishes the absolute negative rule: There has NEVER been a manic episode. If a manic episode ever occurs in the patient's lifetime, the diagnosis immediately converts to Bipolar I Disorder and can never be Bipolar II again.\n\nCriterion C mandates that the occurrence of the hypomanic and major depressive episodes is not better explained by Schizoaffective Disorder or other psychotic spectrum disorders.\n\nCriterion D requires that the symptoms of depression or the unpredictability caused by frequent alternation between periods of depression and hypomania cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning. (Note: While hypomania itself does not cause marked impairment, the depressive episodes or overall illness trajectory do cause impairment).",
        'specifiers' => "Specifiers for Bipolar II Disorder include: 'Current or most recent episode: Hypomanic' or 'Current or most recent episode: Depressed', along with clinical specifiers: 'With anxious distress', 'With mixed features', 'With rapid cycling' (>= 4 episodes in 12 months), 'With melancholic features', 'With atypical features', 'With psychotic features' (applicable ONLY during major depressive episodes), and 'With peripartum onset'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Bipolar II vs. Major Depressive Disorder: In MDD, there has never been a hypomanic or manic episode; in Bipolar II, at least one past hypomanic episode (>= 4 days) has occurred. Thorough collateral history from family members is essential, as patients frequently forget past hypomanic periods.\n\nBipolar II vs. Bipolar I Disorder: If the patient has ever had marked functional impairment during mood elevation, required hospitalization, or had psychotic features during an elevated episode, the diagnosis is Bipolar I, not Bipolar II.\n\nBipolar II vs. Borderline Personality Disorder: BPD mood instability fluctuates hourly in response to interpersonal stressors, whereas Bipolar II hypomanic episodes persist for at least 4 consecutive days independently of immediate social events.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 34-year-old teacher presenting with severe depression, lethargy, and passive suicidal ideation lasting three weeks. The teacher was treated with SSRI antidepressants twice in the past without success. Careful longitudinal clinical interview reveals that three years ago, the teacher had a 5-day period of sleeping only four hours per night, feeling energized, painting twelve canvases, and speaking with rapid enthusiasm.\n\nFamily members confirmed this 5-day period was an obvious, uncharacteristic shift in behavior, but noted the teacher did not miss work, was never hospitalized, and did not experience delusions. The documentation of this past 5-day hypomanic episode confirms that the current depressive presentation represents Bipolar II Disorder, Current Episode Depressed, directing treatment away from antidepressant monotherapy toward evidence-based bipolar depression regimens (e.g., quetiapine, lurasidone, or lamotrigine).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: Bipolar II requires BOTH at least one Hypomanic episode (>= 4 days) AND at least one Major Depressive episode. There has NEVER been a Manic episode; a single manic episode permanently changes the diagnosis to Bipolar I. Psychotic features can occur in Bipolar II ONLY during a Major Depressive episode (never during hypomania). Patients spend the majority of ill time in depression. Antidepressant monotherapy is contraindicated due to risk of inducing mood switching or cycle acceleration.",
