    5 => [
        'title' => 'Substance/Medication-Induced & Secondary Bipolar Conditions',
        'slug' => 'substance-medication-induced-secondary-bipolar',
        'description' => 'Antidepressant-induced mania, corticosteroid toxicity, medical mimics (hyperthyroidism, stroke), and temporal rule-outs.',
        'overview' => "When evaluating acute manic or hypomanic symptoms, clinicians must systematically rule out secondary etiologies arising from pharmacological agents, substances of abuse, or underlying medical and neurological illnesses. Secondary mood elevation can present identically to idiopathic Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder, yet demands targeted medical and toxicological interventions rather than standard psychiatric management.\n\nSubstance/Medication-Induced Bipolar and Related Disorder is diagnosed when manic or hypomanic symptoms emerge during or soon after substance intoxication, substance withdrawal, or after exposure to a medication. The most common pharmacological triggers in clinical practice include systemic corticosteroids (e.g., prednisone-induced mania), prescription stimulants, illicit sympathomimetics (cocaine, amphetamines, methamphetamine), phencyclidine (PCP), and dopaminergic agonists used in Parkinson's disease.\n\nA critically important diagnostic update in DSM-5 addresses antidepressant-induced mood elevation. Under DSM-IV, mania emerging during antidepressant treatment was universally classified as substance-induced. DSM-5 revolutionized this principle: if an individual experiences a full manic or hypomanic episode that persists at a fully syndromal level beyond the physiological effect of the antidepressant treatment, it is considered valid evidence of Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder, recognizing that antidepressants unmask latent genetic bipolar illness.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Substance/Medication-Induced Bipolar Disorder: Criterion A requires a prominent and persistent disturbance in mood characterized by abnormally elevated, expansive, or irritable mood and abnormally increased activity or energy. Criterion B mandates evidence from history, physical exam, or laboratory findings that symptoms developed during or soon after intoxication, withdrawal, or medication exposure, and the substance is capable of producing the symptoms.\n\nCriterion C establishes the crucial exclusion: The disturbance is not better explained by an independent bipolar disorder. Evidence of an independent disorder includes: symptoms preceding substance exposure, symptoms persisting for a substantial period (e.g., > 1 month) after cessation of acute withdrawal or severe intoxication, or other evidence of an independent non-substance-induced bipolar disorder.\n\nFor Bipolar and Related Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition: Criterion A requires a prominent and persistent period of abnormally elevated, expansive, or irritable mood and abnormally increased energy/activity. Criterion B mandates evidence that the disturbance is the direct pathophysiological consequence of another medical condition (e.g., hyperthyroidism, Cushing's syndrome, traumatic brain injury, multiple sclerosis, systemic lupus erythematosus).",
        'specifiers' => "For Substance/Medication-Induced: Clinicians record the specific substance and specify: 'With onset during intoxication' or 'With onset during withdrawal'.\n\nFor Due to Another Medical Condition: Clinicians specify: 'With manic features', 'With manic- or hypomanic-like episode', or 'With mixed features'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Antidepressant-Induced Mania vs. Primary Bipolar Disorder: If manic symptoms remit immediately when the antidepressant is discontinued, it is coded as Substance/Medication-Induced Bipolar Disorder. If the full manic episode persists beyond the expected pharmacological clearance window of the antidepressant, it justifies a formal diagnosis of Bipolar I Disorder.\n\nHyperthyroidism vs. Mania: Thyrotoxicosis produces tachycardia, heat intolerance, weight loss, and psychomotor agitation, which mimics mania; thyroid function panels (TSH, free T4) are mandatory diagnostic rule-outs.\n\nDelirium: If mood elevation occurs in the context of fluctuating awareness and acute disorientation, Delirium is diagnosed.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 42-year-old patient with severe systemic lupus erythematosus started on high-dose prednisone (60 mg daily). Within 72 hours, the patient stopped sleeping, began purchasing luxury goods online, exhibited pressured speech, and experienced grandiose delusions that they had cured their own autoimmune disease.\n\nMedical workup confirms steroid-induced toxicity with no prior psychiatric or mood history. As the corticosteroid dose is tapered under medical supervision, the manic symptoms resolve completely over five days. The accurate diagnosis is Substance/Medication-Induced Bipolar and Related Disorder, with onset during intoxication, due to corticosteroids.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Full manic or hypomanic episodes that persist beyond the physiological clearance of antidepressant treatment are considered evidence of true Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder under DSM-5. Systemic corticosteroids are the classic pharmacological cause of medication-induced mania. Medical conditions that mimic mania include hyperthyroidism, Cushing disease, right hemisphere stroke, and multiple sclerosis. If symptoms occur exclusively during Delirium, Delirium takes diagnostic priority.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The DSM-5 Antidepressant Exception', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Manic episodes persisting beyond the physiological effects of an antidepressant confirm an underlying Bipolar I diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Corticosteroid Mania Pathway', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'High-dose systemic corticosteroids represent the leading medical pharmacological trigger for acute secondary manic presentations.'],
            ['title' => 'Thyroid & Endocrine Mimicry', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Thyrotoxicosis and hypercortisolemia produce elevated energy, insomnia, and agitation that mimic hypomanic states.'],
            ['title' => 'Clearance Window Rule', 'icon' => '⏳', 'definition' => 'Symptoms persisting >1 month after complete drug elimination indicate an independent primary bipolar disorder.']
