        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Steroid Psychosis / Mania', 'definition' => 'A severe neuropsychiatric adverse effect of corticosteroid therapy characterized by affective elevation, grandiosity, insomnia, and occasionally frank delusions.'],
            ['term' => 'Antidepressant-Induced Manic Switch', 'definition' => 'The pharmacological provocation of a manic state in a genetically predisposed individual following administration of serotonergic or noradrenergic antidepressants.'],
            ['term' => 'Thyrotoxicosis', 'definition' => 'A hypermetabolic clinical state resulting from elevated circulating thyroid hormone levels, presenting with hyperadrenergic symptoms mimicking mania.'],
            ['term' => 'Secondary Mania', 'definition' => 'A manic syndrome arising as the direct consequence of a demonstrable medical, neurological, or toxic etiology rather than primary idiopathic psychiatric illness.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Course Specifiers: Rapid Cycling, Anxious Distress, and Melancholic Features',
        'slug' => 'course-specifiers-rapid-cycling-anxious-distress-melancholic',
        'description' => 'The 4-episode annual rapid cycling rule, treatment implications, anxious distress criteria, and melancholic features.',
        'overview' => "Course specifiers in Bipolar and Related Disorders provide critical prognostic, phenotypic, and therapeutic information that enhances standard categorical diagnoses. Bipolar disorder is a remarkably heterogeneous condition; two patients with Bipolar I Disorder may share the same primary diagnosis while demonstrating radically divergent clinical trajectories, relapse risks, and treatment responses based on their course specifiers.\n\nThe 'With rapid cycling' specifier identifies an aggressive, highly challenging course characterized by the occurrence of at least four distinct mood episodes (major depressive, manic, or hypomanic) within a single 12-month period. Rapid cycling occurs in approximately 10% to 20% of bipolar patients, predominantly in women, and is associated with poorer long-term prognosis, higher suicide risk, and diminished responsiveness to lithium monotherapy.\n\nThe 'With anxious distress' specifier was newly introduced in DSM-5 across bipolar and depressive chapters. Historically, anxiety was often treated as an incidental symptom; however, empirical evidence confirms that clinically significant anxiety during mood episodes substantially increases suicide risk, prolongs episode duration, and increases the likelihood of treatment nonresponse.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Specifier Criteria Formulations:\n\n1. With Rapid Cycling: Can be applied to Bipolar I or Bipolar II Disorder. Requires the presence of at least 4 mood episodes in the previous 12 months that meet the criteria for a manic, hypomanic, or major depressive episode. (Note: Episodes are demarcated either by full remission or by a switch to an episode of the opposite polarity; manic and hypomanic episodes are counted as the same polarity).\n\n2. With Anxious Distress: Requires the presence of at least two of the following five symptoms during the majority of days of the current or most recent mood episode: (1) Feeling keyed up or tense; (2) Feeling unusually restless; (3) Difficulty concentrating because of worry; (4) Fear that something awful may happen; (5) Feeling that the individual might lose control of himself or herself. Severity is coded based on symptom count: Mild (2 symptoms), Moderate (3 symptoms), Moderate-severe (4-5 symptoms), and Severe (4-5 symptoms with motor agitation).\n\n3. With Melancholic Features: Can be applied to a major depressive episode. Requires either loss of pleasure in all activities (anhedonia) or lack of reactivity to usually pleasurable stimuli, PLUS at least three of the following: (1) distinct quality of depressed mood (profound despondency); (2) depression regularly worse in the morning; (3) early-morning awakening (at least 2 hours before normal); (4) marked psychomotor agitation or retardation; (5) significant anorexia or weight loss; (6) excessive or inappropriate guilt.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians assign all applicable specifiers to the current or most recent episode, recording severity ratings for anxious distress and documenting course patterns such as rapid cycling, seasonal pattern, and peripartum onset.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Rapid Cycling vs. Mixed Features: In Rapid Cycling, episodes occur sequentially over the course of a year (>= 4 discrete episodes); in Mixed Features, symptoms of both polarities occur simultaneously within the same episode.\n\nRapid Cycling vs. Ultradian Mood Swings in Borderline Personality: In Borderline Personality, mood shifts occur multiple times within hours in response to interpersonal stressors; in Rapid Cycling Bipolar, episodes are full syndromal episodes meeting standard duration criteria.\n\nMelancholic Depression vs. Atypical Depression: Melancholic features involve morning worsening, terminal insomnia, and lack of mood reactivity; Atypical features involve mood reactivity (mood brightens to positive events), hypersomnia, hyperphagia, and leaden paralysis.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 31-year-old patient with Bipolar I Disorder evaluated in clinic. Review of medical records reveals that over the past 11 months, the patient had a 2-week manic episode in January, a 4-week severe depressive episode in April, a 1-week manic episode in July, and has now presented with a major depressive episode in November.\n\nDuring the current depressive episode, the patient reports feeling constantly keyed up, fearful that their family will be destroyed, and unable to sit still from motor tension. Meeting criteria for four distinct mood episodes within a 12-month period establishes the 'With rapid cycling' specifier, and meeting three anxiety symptoms confirms the 'With anxious distress, Moderate' specifier.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Rapid cycling requires at least 4 mood episodes within a 12-month period. Rapid cycling is more common in females and responds poorly to lithium monotherapy; mood stabilizers like valproate or combination therapies are preferred. Anxious distress requires at least 2 of 5 anxiety symptoms during the mood episode and significantly elevates suicide risk. Melancholic features feature terminal early morning awakening and non-reactive mood.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 4-Episode Annual Threshold', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'Requires at least four distinct mood episodes (depressive, manic, or hypomanic) within a single 12-month period.'],
            ['title' => 'Demarcation of Discrete Episodes', 'icon' => '↔️', 'definition' => 'Episodes must be separated by at least 2 months of full remission or by a direct switch to the opposite polarity.'],
            ['title' => 'Anxious Distress Risk Elevation', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Identifies patients with intense anxiety during mood episodes who carry elevated suicide lethality and treatment resistance.'],
            ['title' => 'Melancholic Biological Markers', 'icon' => '🌅', 'definition' => 'Characterized by diurnal mood variation (worse in morning), terminal insomnia, and profound anhedonia.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Rapid Cycling', 'definition' => 'A course specifier in Bipolar I or II Disorder defined by the occurrence of four or more mood episodes within a 12-month period.'],
            ['term' => 'Terminal Insomnia (Early Morning Awakening)', 'definition' => 'Waking up at least two hours before the normal waking time with an inability to fall back asleep, characteristic of melancholic depression.'],
            ['term' => 'Mood Reactivity', 'definition' => 'The capacity for mood to temporarily brighten in response to positive actual or potential external events, absent in melancholic states.'],
            ['term' => 'Diurnal Variation', 'definition' => 'A circadian pattern in mood severity, classically manifesting in melancholia as severe depressive paralysis in the morning with mild improvement by evening.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Mixed Features & Peripartum Onset Specifiers',
        'slug' => 'mixed-features-peripartum-onset-specifiers',
        'description' => 'DSM-5 replacement of mixed episodes, subthreshold counter-polarity criteria, peripartum timing (4-week rule), and postpartum psychosis.',
        'overview' => "The 'With mixed features' specifier represents one of the most substantial conceptual updates in DSM-5. In DSM-IV, a 'Mixed Episode' required that the patient simultaneously satisfy full criteria for both a Manic Episode and a Major Depressive Episode nearly every day for a full week. This historical criterion was excessively restrictive, excluding the vast majority of patients who experienced clinically severe counter-polarity symptoms during mood episodes.\n\nDSM-5 eliminated the standalone Mixed Episode diagnosis and replaced it with the 'With mixed features' specifier, which can be applied to Manic, Hypomanic, or Major Depressive episodes across Bipolar I, Bipolar II, and Major Depressive Disorder. This dimensional specifier requires the presence of at least three subthreshold symptoms of the opposite polarity during the primary mood episode, capturing affective instability and significantly elevated suicide risk.\n\nThe 'With peripartum onset' specifier applies when mood symptoms emerge during pregnancy or within the first four weeks postpartum. The postpartum transition involves massive neuroendocrine shifts—including precipitous drops in estrogen and progesterone, thyroid alterations, and severe sleep disruption—that can trigger acute postpartum mania, depression, or medical emergency postpartum psychosis.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Specifier Formulations under DSM-5:\n\n1. Manic or Hypomanic Episode, With Mixed Features: Full criteria are met for a manic or hypomanic episode, and at least three of the following depressive symptoms are present during the majority of days: (1) Prominent dysphoria or depressed mood; (2) Diminished interest or pleasure (anhedonia); (3) Psychomotor retardation; (4) Fatigue or loss of energy; (5) Feelings of worthlessness or excessive/inappropriate guilt; (6) Recurrent thoughts of death or suicidal ideation.\n\n2. Depressive Episode, With Mixed Features: Full criteria are met for a major depressive episode, and at least three of the following manic/hypomanic symptoms are present during the majority of days: (1) Elevated, expansive mood; (2) Inflated self-esteem or grandiosity; (3) More talkative than usual or pressured speech; (4) Flight of ideas or racing thoughts; (5) Increased goal-directed activity or energy; (6) Increased involvement in high-risk activities; (7) Decreased need for sleep (feels rested despite minimal sleep).\n\n3. With Peripartum Onset: Applied when the current or most recent mood episode occurs during pregnancy or in the 4 weeks following delivery.",
        'specifiers' => "When applying the mixed features specifier, symptoms that are shared across both polarities (such as distractibility, irritability, and insomnia) are deliberately excluded from the counting criteria to ensure that only true counter-polarity symptoms establish the specifier.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Mixed Features vs. Rapid Cycling: In mixed features, counter-polarity symptoms occur simultaneously within the same episode; in rapid cycling, distinct episodes occur sequentially across a 12-month period.\n\nPostpartum Blues vs. Peripartum Major Depression / Mania: 'Postpartum blues' occurs in up to 80% of mothers, peaks around postpartum day 3-5, resolves within 10-14 days, and causes mild emotional lability without functional impairment; peripartum major depression or mania involves severe functional impairment and requires clinical treatment.\n\nPostpartum Psychosis: A psychiatric emergency typically representing a manifestation of Bipolar Disorder, featuring confusion, delirium-like disorientation, infanticide delusions, and rapid manic escalation.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 28-year-old mother two weeks after an uncomplicated childbirth who becomes severely agitated, sleeping only two hours per night without feeling tired. Family members report that while pacing the nursery, the mother alternates between tearful despair declaring she is an unfit mother (dysphoria, guilt) and grandiosely declaring that her child is a prophet destined to rule nations (grandiosity, pressured speech).\n\nThe patient is experiencing a manic episode with concurrent prominent depressive dysphoria, guilt, and suicidal thoughts, satisfying criteria for Bipolar I Disorder, Current Episode Manic, With Mixed Features, With Peripartum Onset.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 replaced the standalone 'Mixed Episode' with the 'With mixed features' specifier, requiring at least 3 symptoms of the opposite polarity. Shared symptoms (irritability, distractibility, insomnia) do NOT count toward mixed features. The peripartum onset specifier requires onset during pregnancy or within 4 weeks postpartum (not 6 months or 1 year). Postpartum psychosis is most frequently a manifestation of underlying Bipolar Disorder and carries severe infanticide and suicide risks.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Mixed Episodes', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Replaced DSM-IV full-episode overlap with a 3-symptom counter-polarity specifier applicable to all mood states.'],
            ['title' => 'Exclusion of Overlapping Symptoms', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Symptoms shared by mania and depression (irritability, agitation, distractibility) cannot be used to meet mixed criteria.'],
            ['title' => 'The 4-Week Peripartum Window', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Restricted strictly to episodes with onset during pregnancy or within the first 28 days postpartum.'],
            ['title' => 'Postpartum Psychosis Emergency', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'An acute psychiatric crisis closely linked to Bipolar Disorder requiring urgent inpatient stabilization to ensure infant safety.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Mixed Features', 'definition' => 'A DSM-5 specifier assigned when a mood episode of one polarity features at least three subthreshold symptoms of the opposite polarity.'],
            ['term' => 'Postpartum Blues', 'definition' => 'A transient, self-limited period of mild emotional lability, tearfulness, and fatigue affecting up to 80% of postpartum women, resolving within two weeks.'],
            ['term' => 'Postpartum Psychosis', 'definition' => 'An acute, severe psychiatric emergency emerging in the early postpartum period characterized by confusion, affective lability, delusions, and hallucinations.'],
            ['term' => 'Dysphoric Mania', 'definition' => 'A manic episode accompanied by prominent depressive feelings, despair, and anguish, clinically categorized under Manic Episode, With Mixed Features.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: Bipolar vs. Major Depression vs. Borderline Personality Disorder vs. ADHD vs. Schizoaffective',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-bipolar-mdd-bpd-adhd-schizoaffective',
        'description' => 'Differential algorithms, clinical boundary distinctions, episodic vs. trait instability, and mistreatment risks.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of Bipolar and Related Disorders is among the most clinically consequential responsibilities in psychiatric practice. Misdiagnosing bipolar disorder can have catastrophic outcomes: misidentifying bipolar depression as unipolar depression leads to antidepressant monotherapy that risks manic switching and cycle acceleration, while misdiagnosing ADHD as bipolar disorder can expose patients to unnecessary mood stabilizer toxicity.\n\nA fundamental boundary distinction lies between Bipolar Disorder and Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD). Both conditions feature emotional lability, impulsivity, and stormy interpersonal relationships. However, in BPD, affective instability manifests as micro-episodes lasting hours (or rarely a few days) that are directly reactive to perceived interpersonal abandonment or rejection. In contrast, Bipolar mood episodes represent sustained, autonomous neurobiological states lasting weeks or months that disrupt sleep architecture and energy levels independently of immediate interpersonal events.\n\nSimilarly, differentiating ADHD from pediatric or adult bipolar disorder requires analyzing temporal patterning: ADHD is a chronic, trait-like, non-episodic neurodevelopmental executive dysfunction present since childhood, whereas Bipolar Disorder is episodic, characterized by distinct departures from baseline functioning featuring grandiosity, decreased need for sleep without fatigue, and pressured speech.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Differential Checklist:\n\n1. Bipolar vs. Unipolar Major Depressive Disorder: Has the patient ever experienced at least one episode of elevated mood and increased energy lasting >= 4 days (hypomania) or >= 7 days / hospitalization (mania)? If yes, the diagnosis is BIPOLAR, never unipolar depression.\n\n2. Bipolar vs. Borderline Personality Disorder: Are mood shifts sustained for weeks/months (Bipolar) or do they fluctuate within hours in response to interpersonal conflict (BPD)? Does the patient exhibit chronic identity disturbance and abandonment fears (BPD)?\n\n3. Bipolar vs. ADHD: Is the inattention and restlessness chronic from childhood without distinct episodes (ADHD) or does it occur in discrete cyclical episodes alongside grandiosity and diminished sleep need (Bipolar)?\n\n4. Bipolar with Psychotic Features vs. Schizoaffective Disorder: Do delusions or hallucinations persist for >= 2 weeks in the complete absence of significant mood symptoms? If yes -> Schizoaffective Disorder. If psychosis occurs ONLY during active mood episodes -> Bipolar I with Psychotic Features.",
        'specifiers' => "When formulating differentials, clinicians record primary diagnoses alongside rule-out considerations and specify whether presentations feature comorbidities (e.g., Bipolar I Disorder comorbid with ADHD; or Bipolar II Disorder with Borderline Personality Disorder).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Bipolar vs. General Medical Conditions: Multiple sclerosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, Wilson disease, and right hemisphere lesions can cause secondary manic syndromes; comprehensive neurological exams are required.\n\nBipolar vs. Substance Use Disorders: Cocaine, methamphetamine, and synthetic cathinone intoxication produce manic-like states; toxicology screening and observation during verified abstinence are required.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 22-year-old college student presenting with severe mood swings. The student reports feeling happy in the morning, intensely enraged in the afternoon after an argument with a romantic partner, and profoundly depressed and suicidal by evening.\n\nThe student exhibits chronic fears of abandonment, unstable self-image, and superficial wrist cutting. However, there has never been a sustained 4-day period of decreased sleep need or uncharacteristic goal-directed energy. The rapid, hourly fluctuations triggered by interpersonal conflict represent Borderline Personality Disorder, NOT Bipolar Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Bipolar mood episodes are episodic and sustained (days/weeks/months); Borderline Personality affective instability is reactive and lasts hours. ADHD is chronic and non-episodic, lacking the grandiosity and decreased sleep need of mania. Schizoaffective disorder requires delusions or hallucinations for >= 2 weeks in the ABSENCE of mood episodes. Antidepressants should never be prescribed as monotherapy in bipolar depression.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Episodic vs. Trait Instability', 'icon' => '📈', 'definition' => 'Bipolar disorder features distinct cyclical episodes lasting weeks; personality disorders feature pervasive lifelong emotional traits.'],
            ['title' => 'Interpersonal Reactivity Boundary', 'icon' => '💔', 'definition' => 'Borderline affective shifts are rapidly reactive to social conflict, whereas bipolar episodes are autonomous neurochemical shifts.'],
            ['title' => 'The Sleep Need Distinguisher', 'icon' => '💤', 'definition' => 'Decreased need for sleep (feeling energetic after 2 hours) is specific to mania/hypomania; ADHD and anxiety feature insomnia with exhaustion.'],
            ['title' => 'Schizoaffective 2-Week Separation', 'icon' => '🪟', 'definition' => 'Psychosis persisting for 14 days without mood disturbance confirms schizoaffective disorder over bipolar with psychosis.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Affective Dysregulation', 'definition' => 'Impaired ability to manage and modulate emotional responses, leading to exaggerated mood fluctuations and behavioral impulsivity.'],
            ['term' => 'Interpersonal Hypersensitivity', 'definition' => 'Extreme vulnerability to perceived criticism, rejection, or abandonment, classically driving mood volatility in Borderline Personality Disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Antidepressant Monotherapy', 'definition' => 'The clinical practice of prescribing an antidepressant without a concurrent mood-stabilizing agent, strictly contraindicated in Bipolar Disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Decreased Need for Sleep', 'definition' => 'A pathognomonic symptom of mania wherein an individual sleeps very few hours yet awakens feeling fully rested and energized.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Analysis: Clinical Formulations and Symptom Dissection',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-analysis-bipolar',
        'description' => 'Clinical vignettes, diagnostic formulation workflows, longitudinal timeline charting, and board examination pearls.',
        'overview' => "Clinical expertise in bipolar spectrum pathology requires mastering longitudinal diagnostic formulation. Because cross-sectional clinical presentations can be profoundly misleading—a manic patient may present in an acute emergency department with disorganized psychosis, while a bipolar II patient presents with standard vegetative depression—the clinician must synthesize lifetime chronological history, family pedigree, and collateral informant reports.\n\nLife-charting methodology represents an indispensable clinical tool. By constructing a visual timeline of an individual's lifetime mood episodes, suicide attempts, pharmacological exposures, and psychosocial stressors, clinicians can accurately map cycle frequency, identify rapid cycling patterns, and detect latent hypomanic switches unmasked by antidepressant trials.\n\nThis educational module analyzes complex case vignettes designed to reflect challenging clinical scenarios and high-yield board examination stems, demonstrating how to apply DSM-5 criteria systematically to arrive at defensible diagnostic conclusions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Formulation Workflow:\n1. Step 1: Establish lifetime presence of Mania. Has the patient ever experienced >= 7 days of elevated/irritable mood + energy, marked impairment, hospitalization, or psychosis? If YES -> Bipolar I Disorder.\n2. Step 2: If no mania, establish lifetime presence of Hypomania (>= 4 days, observable change, no marked impairment) AND Major Depressive Episode (>= 2 weeks). If YES -> Bipolar II Disorder.\n3. Step 3: If criteria for full episodes have never been met, evaluate for Cyclothymic Disorder (>= 2 years of subthreshold oscillations).\n4. Step 4: Apply mandatory course specifiers (Rapid cycling, Anxious distress, Mixed features, Peripartum onset).",
        'specifiers' => "Formulations must document complete multi-axial diagnostic specifiers, including severity grading, remission status, and current episode polarity.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case analyses systematically evaluate: Bipolar I vs. Bipolar II; Bipolar II vs. MDD; Bipolar I vs. Schizoaffective Disorder; and Secondary Mania Due to Medical Conditions.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 38-year-old attorney is admitted to the hospital following an altercation at a luxury car dealership. The attorney had not slept in four days, was speaking at rapid speed, bought five sports cars with company funds, and asserted they had been appointed special advisor to the United Nations.\n\nCollateral history from the attorney's spouse reveals two prior episodes of severe depression lasting several months during college, but no prior episodes of mania. The current presentation satisfies full criteria for a Manic Episode with Grandiose Delusions (Criterion A satisfied, hospitalization required, psychotic features present). Despite the extensive history of prior depressive episodes, the emergence of this single manic episode establishes a lifetime diagnosis of Bipolar I Disorder, Current Episode Manic, Severe, With Psychotic Features.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Always prioritize lifetime history over cross-sectional presentation. A single lifetime manic episode establishes Bipolar I Disorder regardless of depressive history. Collateral informant verification is essential for identifying past hypomanic episodes. Look for decreased need for sleep without fatigue as the hallmark symptom distinguishing mania from anxiety or ADHD.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Longitudinal Timeline Charting', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Constructing a lifetime visual chronology of mood episodes, treatments, and hospitalizations to guide diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Cross-Sectional Diagnostic Trap', 'icon' => '🪤', 'definition' => 'Avoiding the error of diagnosing unipolar depression when evaluating a bipolar patient during an acute depressive phase.'],
            ['title' => 'Collateral Source Triangulation', 'icon' => '👥', 'definition' => 'Obtaining spouse, parent, or family corroboration to identify unrecognized past hypomanic or manic periods.'],
            ['title' => 'Systematic Diagnostic Trees', 'icon' => '🌳', 'definition' => 'Step-by-step application of DSM-5 exclusionary rules and criteria thresholds to build defensible formulations.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Life Charting', 'definition' => 'A systematic clinical method of charting mood episodes, treatment responses, and life events longitudinally across the patient\'s lifespan.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Polarity', 'definition' => 'The affective direction of a mood disturbance, referring to depressive (negative affect) versus manic/hypomanic (elevated affect) presentations.'],
            ['term' => 'Index Episode', 'definition' => 'The specific presenting psychiatric episode that prompts the current clinical evaluation and treatment intervention.'],
            ['term' => 'Kindling Hypothesis', 'definition' => 'The neurobiological theory proposing that initial mood episodes are triggered by external stressors, but over time, repeated episodes alter neural circuitry so subsequent episodes occur autonomously.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Review',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-review-bipolar',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Bipolar and Related Disorders, duration thresholds, diagnostic hierarchies, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit provides a comprehensive synthesis of the Bipolar and Related Disorders chapter under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic distinctions, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Bipolar spectrum disorders represent high-yield content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic operational definitions, specifier criteria, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Bipolar I Disorder, Bipolar II Disorder, Cyclothymic Disorder, and Substance-Induced Bipolar conditions. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the separation of Bipolar Disorders into their own dedicated chapter, the requirement for increased activity/energy in Criterion A, the replacement of Mixed Episodes with the 'With mixed features' specifier, and the antidepressant switch rule.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and risk assessment strategies, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Bipolar Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Bipolar I Disorder: Requires >= 1 lifetime Manic Episode (>= 1 week or requiring hospitalization, causing marked impairment or with psychosis). Major depressive episode is NOT required.\n\n2. Bipolar II Disorder: Requires >= 1 lifetime Hypomanic Episode (>= 4 consecutive days, observable change, NO marked impairment) AND >= 1 lifetime Major Depressive Episode (>= 2 weeks). Absolute lifetime exclusion of Mania.\n\n3. Cyclothymic Disorder: Requires >= 2 years of subthreshold mood fluctuations in adults (>= 1 year in children/adolescents), symptom-free for no more than 2 consecutive months, with no full episodes ever met.\n\n4. Rapid Cycling Specifier: Requires >= 4 mood episodes (depressive, manic, or hypomanic) within a 12-month period.\n\n5. Mixed Features Specifier: Requires >= 3 counter-polarity symptoms during a manic, hypomanic, or depressive episode; shared symptoms (irritability, distractibility) are excluded.\n\n6. Peripartum Onset: Onset during pregnancy or within the first 4 weeks postpartum.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Severity grading (Mild, Moderate, Severe), Psychotic features (Mood-congruent vs. Mood-incongruent), Anxious distress (Mild to Severe), Rapid cycling, Mixed features, Melancholic features, Atypical features, and Peripartum onset.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Mood elevation for 5 days with marked impairment or hospitalization -> MANIC Episode -> Bipolar I Disorder.\n• Mood elevation for 5 days without marked impairment and no hospitalization -> HYPOMANIC Episode -> Bipolar II Disorder.\n• Delusions of grandiosity during mood elevation -> MANIC Episode -> Bipolar I Disorder (Psychosis = Mania).\n• Hallucinations persist 3 weeks after mania resolves -> Schizoaffective Disorder (2-week rule satisfied).\n• Rapid mood swings lasting hours in response to rejection -> Borderline Personality Disorder.\n• Antidepressant-induced mania persisting after medication clearance -> Bipolar I Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 30-year-old patient who has been treated for recurrent major depression since age 20. Over the past six days, the patient has slept only three hours per night, has spent $20,000 on art supplies, feels invincible, and is pacing energetically. The patient has been hospitalized due to aggressive arguments with bank managers.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the accurate diagnosis? The candidate recognizes that the hospitalization and marked impairment immediately establish a Manic Episode, which instantly converts the lifetime diagnosis from Major Depressive Disorder to Bipolar I Disorder, Current Episode Manic, Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: Bipolar I = at least 1 Manic episode (>=1 week or hospitalization). Bipolar II = Hypomania (>=4 days) + Major Depression (>=2 weeks), NO mania. Psychosis during mood elevation = Mania = Bipolar I. Rapid cycling = >=4 episodes in 12 months. DSM-5 elevated activity/energy to mandatory Criterion A status. Antidepressant monotherapy in bipolar depression carries severe risk of inducing mania or rapid cycling.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Bipolar Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all bipolar spectrum disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The Psychosis = Mania Axiom', 'icon' => '👑', 'definition' => 'Psychotic symptoms during mood elevation automatically convert the episode to Mania, establishing Bipolar I.'],
            ['title' => 'Activity/Energy Criterion A Requirement', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Mandatory DSM-5 requirement that mood disturbance must be paired with persistently increased activity or energy.'],
            ['title' => 'Antidepressant Switch Recognition', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Understanding that antidepressant-induced mania that persists beyond drug clearance establishes true Bipolar I Disorder.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Bipolar Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The continuum of mood disorders characterized by pathological mood elevation, ranging from cyclothymia to severe psychotic Bipolar I.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypomanic Threshold', 'definition' => 'The minimum operational criteria required for hypomania: 4 consecutive days of observable change without marked impairment.'],
            ['term' => 'Affective Polarity', 'definition' => 'The clinical distinction between elevated (manic/hypomanic) and depressed emotional states.'],
            ['term' => 'Lithium Carbonate', 'definition' => 'The gold-standard mood-stabilizing medication with proven anti-suicidal properties used in the acute and maintenance treatment of Bipolar Disorder.']
        ]
    ]
];
